<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acropolis of Athens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Acropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> is the rocky citadel above the city, occupied since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and the site of a fortified Mycenaean palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> sacked it in 480 BCE it was rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/propylaea">Propylaea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erechtheion">Erechtheion</a> and the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Nike. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens.jpg" alt="Acropolis of Athens" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giles Laurent · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1029_Acropolis_of_Athens_in_Greece_at_night_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
